  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 PM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread moderate to locally heavy rainfall \nis expected today, with a few thunderstorms possible.\n\nA potent upper-level low digs across the Great Lake region, slowly \nmoving northeasterly and dragging a cold front through most of \nthe area this afternoon with widespread showers with embedded \nthunderstorms. Thunderstorms and heavier rainfall rates have \nsituated over SE VA/NE NC and the lower Eastern Shore. Rainfall \nrates have been very efficient today with multiple localized \nareas seeing rainfall totals of ~2-3\" and a widespread ~0.5-1\" \nacross most of the area. PWAT values are conducive of the heavy \nrainfall with values of ~1.5-1.8\" in the southeast, dropping to \naround 1\" in the piedmont behind the front. Storms continue to \ntrain over SE VA on top of the ~2-4\" already received today, \nresulting in localized 4-6\" totals. A Flood Watch has been \nissued and will remain in effect for SE VA/NE NC through 8 PM \ntonight. The greatest flood threat will be in urban locations as\nthe flood threat will be limited in rural areas due to ongoing \ndrought and low streamflow. Heavy rainfall and localized \nflooding is the primary threat, but cannot rule out a stronger \nstorm in NE NC. MLCAPE values are only ~500 J/kg based on the \nlatest mesoanalysis analysis, but this could lead to a brief \nstrong wind gust with any strong storm. SPC has included NE NC \nin a small Slight Risk, but it appears that the threat is mostly\nconcentrated south of the forecast area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drying out and becoming seasonably cool this \nweekend into early next week.\n\nBehind the cold front, high pressure will build into the area. \nNorthwesterly flow will return as the closed low pressure driving \nthe front moves over the Northeast CONUS. Much cooler and drier air \nis forecasted to advect into the area, with high temperatures \nsettling back to seasonal averages. High temperatures this weekend \nwill be in the low to mid 60s, rising to the mid to upper 60s early \nnext week, with potential for ~70F on Tuesday for a large portion of \nthe area. Tonight's lows will be moderate compared to the next \ncoming days with lows in the 40s W and N to mid 50s SE. Then, Sunday \nmorning's lows will be in the mid to upper 30s inland and low 40s \nalong the coast. Patchy frost is possible, particularly in the \npiedmont and counties that have already ended their growing season. \nWill continue to monitor trends for areas where the growing season \nhas not ended. The recent ensemble guidance is continuing to show \ndry weather persisting into the middle of next week as opposed to \nbringing in any more rain chances.",
